  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 116 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nSunday-Monday...As the two shortwaves quickly move to the \nsoutheast, the deformation zone remain in place Sunday, but \nPoPs should decrease to 30-50% with the lack of upper-level \ndynamical support. The low pressure over the Central Plains is\nalso expected to stall out which will be an issue heading into \nSunday night as another subtle shortwave zooms across Wyoming \ninto South Dakota and Nebraska. A second LLJ is likely to \ndevelop, spawning another round of showers and storms Sunday \nnight into Monday. This shortwave trough will also reinvigorate \nthe deformation zone and pull it northward. The increased \nsouthwesterly flow, low-level WAA, and moisture advection could \nlead to excessive heat developing, especially in our south where\nclearing skies are more likely. Current LREF guidance has a \n20-30% chance of heat indices exceeding 100 degrees south of \nInterstate 80 Monday afternoon. Regardless though, dew points \nare going to be in the 70s, possibly in the mid-to-upper 70s \n(40-60% chance per 06.12z LREF guidance), making it feel muggy \nto oppressive outside even with potential cloud cover.\n\nTuesday-Thursday...Multiple shortwaves passages will keep \nprecipitation chances around through the middle of next week, but \nthese repeated passages should slowly push the deformation zone \nfurther south as high pressure eventually settles back in over\nthe region. There is general consensus among the ensembles that\na return to north-to-northeasterly flow in the low-levels \nshould erode away at the high dew points and temperatures, \nreturning to the area to seasonable conditions by the end of \nnext week.",
